    {
      "id": "yfj6iuo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.1 Governor"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Examines the constitutional roles and executive powers of the Governor, Chief Minister, and Council of Ministers.",
        "Details the organizational structure and legislative procedures of the State Legislature.",
        "Focuses on judicial administration and jurisdiction of the High Court of Andhra Pradesh.",
        "Outlines the framework of District Administration and Local Self-Government institutions.",
        "Includes a comprehensive chronological reference of the Chief Ministers of Andhra Pradesh."
      ],
      "id": "7qnxlwy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eu75zmh",
      "content": "The Governor is the constitutional head of the state, appointed by the President of India. The Governor appoints the Chief Minister and the Council of Ministers on the advice of the Chief Minister. Key powers and responsibilities include summoning and proroguing the legislature, giving assent to bills, making appointments to important offices, and acting as a link between the state and the Central Government under Article 200."
    },
    {
      "id": "04mpaic",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.2 Chief Minister and Council of Ministers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kn18wt9",
      "content": "The Chief Minister is the head of government and leads the Council of Ministers. The Chief Minister enjoys executive powers and is responsible for the administration of the state. The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Vidhan Sabha."
    },
    {
      "id": "30xkxpa",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.3 State Legislature"
    },
    {
